docs {
  # Race Condition Example

  This folder is **not** part of the main, always-green suite - it is excluded from
  the plain recursive run (see the top-level README) and must be run with its own
  special command, because it deliberately provokes the documented, timing-dependent
  duplicate-movie race between two concurrent imports of the same brand-new movie
  (see `ImportWriter`'s class Javadoc in the backend for the full explanation).

  ## How to run the demo

  From `e2e-tests/`, fire two concurrent uploads of `fixtures/race-condition-example.xml`
  using the Bruno CLI's built-in CSV + parallel runner (`race-condition.csv` just
  supplies two rows so the same request runs twice at once; its column is unused):

  ```
  npx @usebruno/cli run "04-Race-Condition-Example/Upload race condition example movie.bru" \
    --env Local \
    --csv-file-path "04-Race-Condition-Example/race-condition.csv" \
    --parallel \
    --output race-condition-report.json --format json
  ```

  ## How to interpret the outcome

  Both requests almost always still get back `202 QUEUED` immediately (upload
  acceptance is synchronous and near-instant); the race is in what happens
  *afterwards*, asynchronously. Open `race-condition-report.json` (or watch the
  console output) for the two `id` values in each response body, then poll each
  one directly, e.g.:

  ```
  curl http://localhost:8080/api/imports/<id-1>
  curl http://localhost:8080/api/imports/<id-2>
  ```

  The common outcome is one task `COMPLETED` (with `recordCount: 1`) and the other
  `FAILED`, with an error mentioning the movie was "being imported concurrently"
  and asking you to re-upload - re-uploading it afterwards (e.g. via the
  "Upload race condition example movie" request on its own, without the parallel
  flag) then completes normally with `recordCount: 0`, since the movie now already
  exists.

  This is a best-effort demonstration, not a deterministic assertion: depending on
  machine load and scheduling, both tasks can occasionally both succeed with one
  of them reporting `recordCount: 0` if they happen not to overlap closely enough
  to race at all. Re-run the demo if that happens and you want to see the failure
  path.

  Afterwards, `GET {{baseUrl}}/movies` will show exactly one
  "E2E Race Condition Example Movie" regardless of the outcome above - the whole point
  of the duplicate check is that the movie is never persisted twice.
}
